      <main id="content"><h1>GaussianLipschitzExponential: mathematical reading route</h1><p>Read the statements and derivations in source order. Every result has its own optional Lean statement and proof. Source assumptions, library reuse and unproved boundaries are kept explicit.</p><ol><li><a href="#decl-autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-probability-gaussianlipschitzexponent-bc3871c5f0">Gaussian Lipschitz first moment and signed exponential domain</a></li><li><a href="#decl-autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-probability-gaussianlipschitzexponent-f38030bf8f">Sharp standard Gaussian concentration for every Lipschitz observable</a></li></ol><section id="decl-autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-probability-gaussianlipschitzexponent-bc3871c5f0"><article class="proof-reader" data-authored-declaration="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential.integrable_and_integrable_exp_centered_of_lipschitz"><div class="eyebrow">Samplinglib mathematical lesson</div><h2>Gaussian Lipschitz first moment and signed exponential domain</h2><p><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential.integrable_and_integrable_exp_centered_of_lipschitz</code> · theorem · <a href="index.html">Teaching coverage</a></p><section class="reader-first-pass" aria-label="First reading"><div class="reader-first-pass-label">Read this first</div><h3>Mathematical statement</h3><p class="reader-claim">For every actual Gaussian mu and globally L-Lipschitz scalar f on the stated Banach/Borel space, f is Bochner integrable and exp(t*(f-int f dmu)) is integrable for every real t. This is a Gaussian-domain background theorem, not the sharp concentration bound.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation reader-main-equation">\[f\in L^1(\mu),\qquad e^{t(f-\int f\,d\mu)}\in L^1(\mu)\quad(t\in\mathbb R).\]</div><h4>Proof roadmap</h4><ol class="proof-roadmap"><li><span>1</span><strong>Produce the genuine first moment</strong><p>Lipschitz growth bounds the actual scalar observable by L times the norm plus its value at zero. Gaussian first Bochner integrability from pinned Mathlib yields norm L1. Continuity gives measurability, so domination yields scalar L1 and a genuine finite mean.</p></li><li><span>2</span><strong>Use actual Fernique integrability</strong><p>Pinned Gaussian Fernique gives one actual C&gt;0 with exp(C norm(x)^2) integrable. The space is a complete second-countable real Banach Borel space. The Gaussian need not be centered or nondegenerate; zero dimensions and zero Lipschitz constant require no extra branch or premise.</p></li><li><span>3</span><strong>Dominate every signed centered exponential</strong><p>For each real t set b=abs(t)L and D=abs(t)(abs(f(0))+abs(m)). Young inequality gives b norm(x)&lt;=C norm(x)^2+C^-1 b^2. Thus the centered exponential is dominated by a finite scalar times the genuine Fernique integrand. This proves the domain only; its constant depends on C and f(0), and supplies no sharp concentration coefficient.</p></li></ol></section><details class="reader-ledger assumption-ledger"><summary>Full assumptions and notation</summary><h4>All objects and hypotheses</h4><ul><li>Real complete second-countable normed vector space E with Borel measurable structure, Gaussian probability measure mu (possibly noncentered/degenerate).</li><li>Actual globally Lipschitz scalar function f:E-&gt;R with NNReal constant L, including L=0. No differentiability, boundedness, positive dimension, given mean, Laplace certificate or sharp coefficient premise.</li></ul><p class="muted"></p></details><h3>Mathematical proof</h3><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>1. Produce the genuine first moment</h5><p>Lipschitz growth bounds the actual scalar observable by L times the norm plus its value at zero. Gaussian first Bochner integrability from pinned Mathlib yields norm L1. Continuity gives measurability, so domination yields scalar L1 and a genuine finite mean.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[|f(x)|\le L\|x\|+|f(0)|,\quad f\in L^1(\mu),\quad m=\int f\,d\mu.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>IsGaussian.integrable_id; LipschitzWith.dist_le_mul; Integrable.mono&#x27;</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>2. Use actual Fernique integrability</h5><p>Pinned Gaussian Fernique gives one actual C&gt;0 with exp(C norm(x)^2) integrable. The space is a complete second-countable real Banach Borel space. The Gaussian need not be centered or nondegenerate; zero dimensions and zero Lipschitz constant require no extra branch or premise.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[\exists C&gt;0:\quad\int e^{C\|x\|^2}\,d\mu(x)&lt;\infty.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>ProbabilityTheory.IsGaussian.exists_integrable_exp_sq</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>3. Dominate every signed centered exponential</h5><p>For each real t set b=abs(t)L and D=abs(t)(abs(f(0))+abs(m)). Young inequality gives b norm(x)&lt;=C norm(x)^2+C^-1 b^2. Thus the centered exponential is dominated by a finite scalar times the genuine Fernique integrand. This proves the domain only; its constant depends on C and f(0), and supplies no sharp concentration coefficient.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[b=|t|L,\ D=|t|(|f(0)|+|m|),\quad e^{t(f(x)-m)}\le e^{D+C^{-1}b^2}e^{C\|x\|^2}.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>two_mul_le_add_mul_sq; Real.exp_le_exp; Integrable.const_mul; Integrable.mono&#x27;</p></details></div><section class="lean-verification"><h3>Lean verification</h3><p>The exact proposition and proof begin folded so the mathematical argument remains the primary reading path.</p><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-statement" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential.integrable_and_integrable_exp_centered_of_lipschitz"><summary>Lean statement · integrable_and_integrable_exp_centered_of_lipschitz</summary><p>For every actual Gaussian mu and globally L-Lipschitz scalar f on the stated Banach/Borel space, f is Bochner integrable and exp(t*(f-int f dmu)) is integrable for every real t. This is a Gaussian-domain background theorem, not the sharp concentration bound.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem integrable_and_integrable_exp_centered_of_lipschitz
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E]
    [CompleteSpace E] [SecondCountableTopology E] [MeasurableSpace E] [BorelSpace E]
    {μ : MeasureTheory.Measure E} [ProbabilityTheory.IsGaussian μ]
    {L : ℝ≥0} {f : E → ℝ} (hf : LipschitzWith L f) :
    MeasureTheory.Integrable f μ ∧ ∀ t : ℝ,
      MeasureTheory.Integrable
        (fun x =&gt; Real.exp (t * (f x - ∫ z, f z ∂μ))) μ</code></pre><p><a href="../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-probability-gaussianlipschitzexponential.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-proof" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential.integrable_and_integrable_exp_centered_of_lipschitz"><summary>Lean proof · integrable_and_integrable_exp_centered_of_lipschitz</summary><p>Produce the genuine first moment; Use actual Fernique integrability; Dominate every signed centered exponential</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem integrable_and_integrable_exp_centered_of_lipschitz
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E]
    [CompleteSpace E] [SecondCountableTopology E] [MeasurableSpace E] [BorelSpace E]
    {μ : MeasureTheory.Measure E} [ProbabilityTheory.IsGaussian μ]
    {L : ℝ≥0} {f : E → ℝ} (hf : LipschitzWith L f) :
    MeasureTheory.Integrable f μ ∧ ∀ t : ℝ,
      MeasureTheory.Integrable
        (fun x =&gt; Real.exp (t * (f x - ∫ z, f z ∂μ))) μ := by
  have hbound (x : E) : |f x| ≤ (L : ℝ)*‖x‖+|f 0| := by
    have hd := hf.dist_le_mul x 0
    simp only [dist_eq_norm, sub_zero, Real.norm_eq_abs] at hd
    calc
      |f x| = |(f x-f 0)+f 0| := by congr 1; ring
      _ ≤ |f x-f 0|+|f 0| := abs_add_le _ _
      _ ≤ (L : ℝ)*‖x‖+|f 0| := add_le_add hd le_rfl
  have hi : Integrable f μ :=
    (((IsGaussian.integrable_id (μ := μ)).norm.const_mul (L : ℝ)).add
      (integrable_const |f 0|)).mono&#x27; hf.continuous.aestronglyMeasurable
        (ae_of_all _ fun x =&gt; by simpa only [Real.norm_eq_abs, Pi.add_apply, id_eq] using hbound x)
  refine ⟨hi,fun t =&gt; ?_⟩
  obtain ⟨C,hC,hint⟩ := IsGaussian.exists_integrable_exp_sq μ
  let b : ℝ := |t| *(L : ℝ)
  let D : ℝ := |t| *(|f 0|+|∫ z, f z ∂μ|)
  have hb : 0 ≤ b := mul_nonneg (abs_nonneg _) L.coe_nonneg
  have hdom (x : E) : t*(f x-∫ z, f z ∂μ) ≤
      D+C⁻¹*b^2+C*‖x‖^2 := by
    have hlin : t*(f x-∫ z, f z ∂μ) ≤ b*‖x‖+D := by
      calc
        _ ≤ |t*(f x-∫ z, f z ∂μ)| := le_abs_self _
        _ = |t| *|f x-∫ z, f z ∂μ| := abs_mul _ _
        _ ≤ |t| *(|f x|+|∫ z, f z ∂μ|) :=
          mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_left (by simpa only [sub_zero, zero_sub, abs_neg] using
            (abs_sub_le (f x) 0 (∫ z, f z ∂μ))) (abs_nonneg _)
        _ ≤ |t| *((L : ℝ)*‖x‖+|f 0|+|∫ z, f z ∂μ|) :=
          mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_left (add_le_add (hbound x) le_rfl) (abs_nonneg _)
        _ = b*‖x‖+D := by dsimp [b,D]; ring
    have hy : 2*‖x‖*b ≤ C*‖x‖^2+C⁻¹*b^2 := two_mul_le_add_mul_sq hC
    have hnon : 0 ≤ b*‖x‖ := mul_nonneg hb (norm_nonneg _)
    nlinarith
  apply (hint.const_mul (Real.exp (D+C⁻¹*b^2))).mono&#x27;
    (by fun_prop)
  filter_upwards with x
  rw [Real.norm_eq_abs,abs_of_pos (Real.exp_pos _),← Real.exp_add]
  exact Real.exp_le_exp.mpr (hdom x)

/- The zero-Lipschitz branch of the sealed sharp target is internal; this is
not an assertion of the remaining positive-dimensional concentration bound. -/</code></pre><p><a href="../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-probability-gaussianlipschitzexponential.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details></section><details class="reader-ledger boundary-ledger"><summary>Scope and omitted-condition boundaries</summary><ul><li>This unchanged general Gaussian domain leaf produces the actual first moment and every signed centered exponential L1; it does not itself supply a sharp concentration coefficient. The separate current finite-Hilbert standard-Gaussian theorem integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz and actual proximal oracle prove the scoped sharp (4.3) bound locally, with independent admission stages recorded separately. Bias (4.2), first W2/LSI/T2 bound (4.6), full Lemma4.2, higher smoothing/Picard/Wp/proxy-warmness/initialization, PBPS implementation/work, both main results and actual-input expected-query-cost composition remain open. TV proximity does not transfer unbounded expected cost.</li></ul></details><details class="proof-reader-provenance reader-ledger"><summary>Sources, dependencies, and reuse</summary><h3>Source and reuse</h3><h4>Samplinglib parents called</h4><ul></ul><h4>Mathlib API called (external library)</h4><ul><li>ProbabilityTheory.IsGaussian.integrable_id</li><li>ProbabilityTheory.IsGaussian.exists_integrable_exp_sq</li><li>LipschitzWith.dist_le_mul</li><li>Integrable.mono&#x27;</li><li>two_mul_le_add_mul_sq</li></ul><h4>Mathematical sources</h4><ul><li><a href="https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/db584cd6d46c92f209a44c0f1c829460d327499d/Mathlib/Probability/Distributions/Gaussian/Fernique.lean">Pinned Gaussian domain background; sharp coefficient supplied by separate declaration</a> — This unchanged general Gaussian domain leaf produces the actual first moment and every signed centered exponential L1; it does not itself supply a sharp concentration coefficient. The separate current finite-Hilbert standard-Gaussian theorem integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz and actual proximal oracle prove the scoped sharp (4.3) bound locally, with independent admission stages recorded separately. Bias (4.2), first W2/LSI/T2 bound (4.6), full Lemma4.2, higher smoothing/Picard/Wp/proxy-warmness/initialization, PBPS implementation/work, both main results and actual-input expected-query-cost composition remain open. TV proximity does not transfer unbounded expected cost.</li></ul><p>Samplinglib prose is not a quotation or a source-equivalence certificate. Definitions and aliases are explained as constructions, not counted as new mathematical proofs.</p></details></article></section><section id="decl-autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-probability-gaussianlipschitzexponent-f38030bf8f"><article class="proof-reader" data-authored-declaration="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential.integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz"><div class="eyebrow">Samplinglib mathematical lesson</div><h2>Sharp standard Gaussian concentration for every Lipschitz observable</h2><p><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential.integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz</code> · theorem · <a href="index.html">Teaching coverage</a></p><section class="reader-first-pass" aria-label="First reading"><div class="reader-first-pass-label">Read this first</div><h3>Mathematical statement</h3><p class="reader-claim">For every globally L-Lipschitz scalar observable on the stated finite real Hilbert/Borel space, its actual standard Gaussian centered exponential integral is at most exp(L^2*t^2/2) for every signed real t. Actual first moments and all exponential domains are produced by the existing shared domain theorem; the C1 restriction used inside the proof is removed internally.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation reader-main-equation">\[\gamma=\operatorname{stdGaussian}(E),\quad m=\int f\,d\gamma,\quad\int e^{t(f-m)}\,d\gamma\le e^{L^2t^2/2}\quad(t\in\mathbb R).\]</div><h4>Proof roadmap</h4><ol class="proof-roadmap"><li><span>1</span><strong>Establish the actual Gaussian domains</strong><p>The existing Fernique-domain declaration proves first Bochner integrability and every signed centered exponential L1 from global Lipschitz growth. Shifting the center gives radial exponential domains. Polynomial factors are dominated by a larger radial exponential, also on the actual product Gaussian equipped with its Banach max norm.</p></li><li><span>2</span><strong>Derive Gaussian Stein integration by parts</strong><p>For the temporary C1 case, the canonical ASTIS Gaussian density with respect to the given Hilbert volume has derivative -w(x) inner(x,-). Growth, the genuine Gaussian second moment and the derivative bound by L prove every Haar integration-by-parts integrability input. The resulting Stein equality has no supplied domain certificate.</p></li><li><span>3</span><strong>Rotate two independent standard Gaussians</strong><p>For X,Y independently distributed as gamma, U=cos(theta)X+sin(theta)Y and W=-sin(theta)X+cos(theta)Y have the same product law. Differentiate F(theta)=E[f(X) exp(t(f(U)-m))] using an explicit polynomial times radial-exponential domination valid for every theta. The inverse relation X=cos(theta)U-sin(theta)W is exact.</p></li><li><span>4</span><strong>Apply Stein only to the affine Lipschitz factor</strong><p>After rotation, fix U=u and let a(u) be the Riesz representative of Df(u). Apply Stein in W to the C1 Lipschitz function W-&gt;f(cu-sW). Its derivative is -s Df(cu-sW). The exponential is constant in this conditional variable; it is never asserted globally Lipschitz. Actual product L1 and Fubini justify the conditional calculation.</p></li><li><span>5</span><strong>Recover the covariance bound with correct sign</strong><p>The inner integral has absolute value at most L squared. On theta in [0,pi/2] and t&gt;=0 the factor -sin(theta)t is nonpositive, so the upper bound on the inner integral gives a lower bound on F prime. For C=t L squared M, H=F-cos(theta)C is nondecreasing by the mean-value theorem. Actual derivative existence gives endpoint continuity. Endpoint independence yields the covariance inequality.</p></li><li><span>6</span><strong>Prove the sharp Herbst estimate and both signs</strong><p>All signed exponential domains give integrableExpSet=the full real line. Pinned Mathlib MGF differentiation gives the actual M prime. The covariance estimate implies M prime&lt;=t L squared M on t&gt;=0. The integrating factor exp(-L squared t squared/2)M is nonincreasing and equals one at zero. Apply the result to -f and -t for negative parameters.</p></li><li><span>7</span><strong>Smooth internally without changing the constant</strong><p>Use the actual nonnegative normalized compactly supported smooth bump against canonical Hilbert volume. Its outer radius is epsilon_n=2^-n and its mass is one. The convolution f_n is C1 and remains globally L Lipschitz. The uniform error is at most L epsilon_n. This introduces no differentiability assumption on the public observable.</p></li><li><span>8</span><strong>Pass the actual center and exponential integral to the limit</strong><p>First-moment integrability and the uniform approximation give |m_n-m|&lt;=L epsilon_n. The centered exponents converge pointwise, and every n is dominated by exp(2|t|L) exp(t(f-m)), whose L1 was proved before the sharp estimate. Dominated convergence passes the same sharp bound to f. Zero L and the zero-dimensional carrier are discharged directly as constant observables.</p></li></ol></section><details class="reader-ledger assumption-ledger"><summary>Full assumptions and notation</summary><h4>All objects and hypotheses</h4><ul><li>Finite-dimensional complete real Hilbert space E with its actual inner product and Borel measurable structure, including dimension zero; gamma is the canonical covariance-identity standard Gaussian.</li><li>Actual globally L-Lipschitz f:E-&gt;R with NNReal L&gt;=0 including zero; every signed t in R. No differentiability, boundedness, given mean, positive dimension or concentration/LSI/domain certificate.</li></ul><p class="muted"></p></details><h3>Mathematical proof</h3><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>1. Establish the actual Gaussian domains</h5><p>The existing Fernique-domain declaration proves first Bochner integrability and every signed centered exponential L1 from global Lipschitz growth. Shifting the center gives radial exponential domains. Polynomial factors are dominated by a larger radial exponential, also on the actual product Gaussian equipped with its Banach max norm.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[m=\int f\,d\gamma,\quad e^{t(f-m)}\in L^1(\gamma),\quad (1+\|x\|)^2e^{a\|x\|}\in L^1(\gamma).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>integrable_and_integrable_exp_centered_of_lipschitz; gaussian_radial_exponential_integrable; gaussian_polynomial_radial_integrable</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>2. Derive Gaussian Stein integration by parts</h5><p>For the temporary C1 case, the canonical ASTIS Gaussian density with respect to the given Hilbert volume has derivative -w(x) inner(x,-). Growth, the genuine Gaussian second moment and the derivative bound by L prove every Haar integration-by-parts integrability input. The resulting Stein equality has no supplied domain certificate.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[w(x)=(\sqrt{2\pi})^{-d}e^{-\|x\|^2/2},\quad Dw(x)[v]=-w(x)\langle x,v\rangle,\quad\int\langle x,v\rangle f(x)\,d\gamma=\int Df(x)[v]\,d\gamma.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>standardGaussian_density; standardWeight_hasFDerivAt; standardGaussian_stein_lipschitz_c1; integral_mul_fderiv_eq_neg_fderiv_mul_of_integrable</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>3. Rotate two independent standard Gaussians</h5><p>For X,Y independently distributed as gamma, U=cos(theta)X+sin(theta)Y and W=-sin(theta)X+cos(theta)Y have the same product law. Differentiate F(theta)=E[f(X) exp(t(f(U)-m))] using an explicit polynomial times radial-exponential domination valid for every theta. The inverse relation X=cos(theta)U-sin(theta)W is exact.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[U_\theta=cX+sY,\ W_\theta=-sX+cY,\quad F^{\prime}(\theta)=t\,\mathbb E[f(X)e^{t(f(U_\theta)-m)}Df(U_\theta)[W_\theta]].\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>standardGaussian_rotation_integral; rotX_hasDerivAt; rotated_laplace_integral_hasDerivAt; rotation_inverse_first</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>4. Apply Stein only to the affine Lipschitz factor</h5><p>After rotation, fix U=u and let a(u) be the Riesz representative of Df(u). Apply Stein in W to the C1 Lipschitz function W-&gt;f(cu-sW). Its derivative is -s Df(cu-sW). The exponential is constant in this conditional variable; it is never asserted globally Lipschitz. Actual product L1 and Fubini justify the conditional calculation.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[F^{\prime}(\theta)=-s t\int e^{t(f(u)-m)}\!\left[\int Df(cu-sv)[a(u)]\,d\gamma(v)\right]d\gamma(u),\quad\|a(u)\|\le L.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>standardGaussian_stein_affine; affine_stein_laplace_slice; rotated_laplace_derivative_integral_identity</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>5. Recover the covariance bound with correct sign</h5><p>The inner integral has absolute value at most L squared. On theta in [0,pi/2] and t&gt;=0 the factor -sin(theta)t is nonpositive, so the upper bound on the inner integral gives a lower bound on F prime. For C=t L squared M, H=F-cos(theta)C is nondecreasing by the mean-value theorem. Actual derivative existence gives endpoint continuity. Endpoint independence yields the covariance inequality.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[M=\int e^{t(f-m)}d\gamma,\quad F^{\prime}\ge-\sin\theta\,tL^2M,\quad H^{\prime}\ge0,\quad\operatorname{Cov}_\gamma(f,e^{t(f-m)})\le tL^2M.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>affine_directional_derivative_integral_norm_le; rotated_laplace_derivative_integral_lower_bound; gaussian_laplace_covariance_bound; monotoneOn_of_hasDerivWithinAt_nonneg</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>6. Prove the sharp Herbst estimate and both signs</h5><p>All signed exponential domains give integrableExpSet=the full real line. Pinned Mathlib MGF differentiation gives the actual M prime. The covariance estimate implies M prime&lt;=t L squared M on t&gt;=0. The integrating factor exp(-L squared t squared/2)M is nonincreasing and equals one at zero. Apply the result to -f and -t for negative parameters.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[M^{\prime}(t)\le tL^2M(t),\quad\left(e^{-L^2t^2/2}M(t)\right)^{\prime}\le0,\quad M(t)\le e^{L^2t^2/2}\quad(t\in\mathbb R).\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>gaussian_centered_integrableExpSet; gaussian_centered_laplace_hasDerivAt; gaussian_centered_laplace_bound_c1; hasDerivAt_mgf; antitoneOn_of_hasDerivWithinAt_nonpos</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>7. Smooth internally without changing the constant</h5><p>Use the actual nonnegative normalized compactly supported smooth bump against canonical Hilbert volume. Its outer radius is epsilon_n=2^-n and its mass is one. The convolution f_n is C1 and remains globally L Lipschitz. The uniform error is at most L epsilon_n. This introduces no differentiability assumption on the public observable.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[f_n=\phi_n*f,\quad\phi_n\ge0,\quad\int\phi_n\,dx=1,\quad\operatorname{Lip}(f_n)\le L,\quad\|f_n-f\|_\infty\le L2^{-n}.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>normed_bump_convolution_lipschitz; normed_bump_convolution_error; smoothApprox_contDiff; smoothApprox_lipschitz</p></details></div><div class="proof-reader-step"><h5>8. Pass the actual center and exponential integral to the limit</h5><p>First-moment integrability and the uniform approximation give |m_n-m|&lt;=L epsilon_n. The centered exponents converge pointwise, and every n is dominated by exp(2|t|L) exp(t(f-m)), whose L1 was proved before the sharp estimate. Dominated convergence passes the same sharp bound to f. Zero L and the zero-dimensional carrier are discharged directly as constant observables.</p><div class="proof-reader-equation">\[|m_n-m|\le L2^{-n},\quad e^{t(f_n-m_n)}\le e^{2|t|L}e^{t(f-m)},\quad\int e^{t(f-m)}d\gamma\le e^{L^2t^2/2}.\]</div><details><summary>Corresponding Lean step</summary><p>smoothApprox_mean_error; smoothApprox_centered_exp_integral_tendsto; centered_exp_integral_of_lipschitz_zero; centered_exp_integral_of_subsingleton; integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz</p></details></div><section class="lean-verification"><h3>Lean verification</h3><p>The exact proposition and proof begin folded so the mathematical argument remains the primary reading path.</p><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-statement" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential.integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz"><summary>Lean statement · integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz</summary><p>For every globally L-Lipschitz scalar observable on the stated finite real Hilbert/Borel space, its actual standard Gaussian centered exponential integral is at most exp(L^2*t^2/2) for every signed real t. Actual first moments and all exponential domains are produced by the existing shared domain theorem; the C1 restriction used inside the proof is removed internally.</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E]
    [CompleteSpace E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] [MeasurableSpace E] [BorelSpace E]
    {L : ℝ≥0} {f : E → ℝ} (hf : LipschitzWith L f) (t : ℝ) :
    (∫ x, Real.exp (t * (f x - ∫ z, f z ∂ProbabilityTheory.stdGaussian E))
      ∂ProbabilityTheory.stdGaussian E) ≤ Real.exp ((L : ℝ)^2 * t^2 / 2)</code></pre><p><a href="../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-probability-gaussianlipschitzexponential.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details><details class="inline-lean inline-lean-proof" data-inline-lean="AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential.integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz"><summary>Lean proof · integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz</summary><p>Establish the actual Gaussian domains; Derive Gaussian Stein integration by parts; Rotate two independent standard Gaussians; Apply Stein only to the affine Lipschitz factor; Recover the covariance bound with correct sign; Prove the sharp Herbst estimate and both signs; Smooth internally without changing the constant; Pass the actual center and exponential integral to the limit</p><p>Braces mark parameters Lean can infer; square brackets request structures such as a measurable space or probability measure. Named hypotheses are mathematical premises, not facts established by this declaration. Section parameters are described in the mathematical hypotheses above; the module link retains their exact source context.</p><pre class="code-block"><code class="language-lean">theorem integral_exp_centered_le_stdGaussian_of_lipschitz
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E]
    [CompleteSpace E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] [MeasurableSpace E] [BorelSpace E]
    {L : ℝ≥0} {f : E → ℝ} (hf : LipschitzWith L f) (t : ℝ) :
    (∫ x, Real.exp (t * (f x - ∫ z, f z ∂ProbabilityTheory.stdGaussian E))
      ∂ProbabilityTheory.stdGaussian E) ≤ Real.exp ((L : ℝ)^2 * t^2 / 2) := by
  by_cases hL : L = 0
  · subst L
    rw [centered_exp_integral_of_lipschitz_zero hf t]
    norm_num
  · rcases subsingleton_or_nontrivial E with hE | hE
    · letI : Subsingleton E := hE
      rw [centered_exp_integral_of_subsingleton f t]
      exact Real.one_le_exp_iff.mpr (by positivity)
    · exact le_of_tendsto&#x27; (smoothApprox_centered_exp_integral_tendsto hf t)
        (fun n =&gt; gaussian_centered_laplace_bound_c1
          (smoothApprox_contDiff hf n) (smoothApprox_lipschitz hf n) t)

end AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential
end</code></pre><p><a href="../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-probability-gaussianlipschitzexponential.html#complete-module-source">Exact module and namespace context</a></p></details></section><details class="reader-ledger boundary-ledger"><summary>Scope and omitted-condition boundaries</summary><ul><li>Locally compiled sharp centered Gaussian-output estimate (4.3), retained exact positive-step proximal/evaluator and (4.4)-(4.5), genuine first Bochner moments and all signed exponential domains. Independent mathematics/blind/source/publication/exact-commit/integration admission pending. Bias (4.2), first W2 inequality in (4.6), LSI/T2 adapter, full Lemma4.2, higher smoothing/Picard/Wp/proxy-warmness/initialization, PBPS invariance/nonexplosion/hypocoercivity/implementation/work, both main results and actual-input precision/expected-query-cost composition remain open. Centers are actual Gaussian output means. TV proximity does not transfer unbounded expected cost.</li></ul></details><details class="proof-reader-provenance reader-ledger"><summary>Sources, dependencies, and reuse</summary><h3>Source and reuse</h3><h4>Samplinglib parents called</h4><ul><li><a href="../theorems/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-probability-gaussianlipschitzexponential-integrable-and-integrable-exp-centered-of-lipschitz.html"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.GaussianLipschitzExponential.integrable_and_integrable_exp_centered_of_lipschitz</code></a></li><li><a href="../modules/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-measure-isotropicgaussiandensity.html#decl-autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-measure-isotropicgaussiandensity-map--958a008371"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Measure.IsotropicGaussianDensity.map_sqrt_smul_stdGaussian_eq_withDensity</code></a></li><li><a href="../theorems/autosamplingtheory-technicallemmas-probability-stdgaussianmoment-integrable-norm-sq-and-integral-stdgaussian.html"><code>AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.Probability.Std